  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 1217 PM MDT Tue Aug 11 2026\n\nPWATs continue to climb across the region from Thursday into\nFriday, rising up above the 90th percentile for mid August and\npotentially approaching calendar day records across central and\nwestern NM. The synoptic setup for Thu/Fri is the upper high over\nthe upper Gulf Coast states and troughing along the west coast, \nallowing for a continued feed of moisture into NM from the south. \nStorms will be moving at a decent clip Thu/Fri from SW to NE given\n700-500mb layer mean wind speeds of 20 mph, which could limit the\nflash flood threat somewhat, but with the high PWAT atmosphere \nand potential for training storms the threat won't be totally \nconfined to area burn scars. Similar overall setup going into \nSaturday and with increasingly wet antecedent conditions, flash \nflooding may be easier to produce. This is a \"Monsoon burst\"-type\npattern that should translate into notable/significant and much-\nneeded rainfall in buckets over several days. Temperatures will \ntrend down during the Thu-Sat period and be near/below average \nacross western NM for a welcomed change. The upper high is \nforecast to build back toward the west over the area beginning \nSunday and continuing through early next week, bringing a gradual \ndowntrend in daily rounds of storms and a bump in temperatures \nback to above average most areas by Tuesday.",
